XINIM builds the pinned dietlibc tree with the same Clang C driver used by
the kernel and project-owned userland. The optional `cross-elf` lane adds a
target triple to that driver; target binutils provide only archiving, GNU
assembly syntax, stripping, preprocessing, and ELF inspection.

The build passes `-fno-integrated-as` for the vendor assembly sources so the
GNU assembler syntax remains supported. The builder injects the exact
configured binutils paths into the copied vendor Makefile; it does not use
ambient host tools.

The pinned tree contains the GCC-only
`__attribute__((optimize("no-stack-protector")))` annotation in
`lib/stackgap-common.h`. Clang already receives `-fno-stack-protector`, so
the vendor-only `-Wno-unknown-attributes` flag keeps this redundant annotation
from failing the vendor build. The same narrow compatibility flags disable
Clang's `-Wdeprecated-non-prototype` diagnostic for the tree's legacy K&R
definitions. XINIM-owned sources retain `-Werror` without these vendor
exceptions.
The vendor-only `-Wno-int-in-bool-context` flag likewise covers an upstream
syslog bit-mask expression whose integer truth conversion is intentional.
The vendor-only `-Wno-null-pointer-subtraction` flag covers the tree's
pointer-to-integer alignment idiom in the cryptographic routines.
The vendor-only `-Wno-switch` flag preserves the upstream regex parser's
intentional fall-through handling for its sentinel enum value.
The vendor-only `-fno-builtin-bcmp` flag preserves dietlibc's weak `bcmp`
alias next to `memcmp`; Clang otherwise treats the legacy alias declaration as
a builtin redeclaration and omits the symbol from the archive.
